{
	title: 'Accept irssi-style literal slash prompts',
	spec: 'The server treats text beginning with slash followed by one ASCII space as a literal prompt: strip exactly the first two characters, once, without parsing the result as a command. Thus / hello becomes hello, / /hello becomes /hello, and /  /hello becomes a space followed by /hello. Text with leading whitespace remains unchanged and is not a slash command. Terminal and web client-only command parsing follows the same column-zero rule. Clients send the original text through the unchanged submit wire message. Restore the invariant that leading whitespace protects slash text from command execution. Stored prompts lose the escape, so src/common/literal.ts escapes a stored prompt again wherever it returns to the editor (input recall, editing the last or an earlier prompt, waiting and queued message edits): text that would parse as a command or that starts with slash-space gets the slash-space prefix. Amend and inbox edits strip it like submit. Commands recall unchanged.',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Server stripping is one startsWith/slice line after command dispatch. Host parsing and client-only parsing no longer discard leading whitespace. Focused host tests cover exactly-once stripping and preserved spaces; terminal tests confirm original text reaches submit. No protocol fields, loops, or state were added.',
		'Bug fix 2026-10-14: recalling or editing a stored prompt that began with /word dropped the escape and ran it as a command (/go printed no session <text>). Invariant restored: text taken from a stored prompt back to the editor sends as the same prompt again.'
	],
	needs: ['yq'],
	foldInto: ['yq']
}
